<description>
Complete the foreground pattern by rotating it into a square with four-way rotational symmetry.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background and a partial foreground pattern inside one hidden square region.
- The intended square is the unique smallest square containing all foreground pixels whose 90-degree rotation closure has no color conflict.

Rule
- Keep the grid shape and background.
- For each foreground pixel in the inferred square, paint its positions under 0, 90, 180, and 270 degree rotations about the square center.

Key insight
The missing cells are not mirrored; they are exactly the orbit needed to complete quarter-turn rotational symmetry.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "foreground pixels inside the unique smallest square whose quarter-turn rotation closure has no color conflict",
  "dst": "missing cells of that same square under 90-degree rotations about its center",
  "transform": "paint each foreground pixel's 0, 90, 180, and 270 degree rotation positions; keep the grid shape and background elsewhere"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10x10, background 0
  colors: exactly 2 non-bg foreground colors (body + accent)
  rotation_square:
    side: 4-7 (odd or even)
    positioned anywhere within the 10x10 grid with >=1 cell margin on all sides [observed]
    content: C4-rotationally-symmetric pattern (invariant under 90-degree rotation)
    fill: 50-75% of square cells are foreground
  orbits:
    each C4 orbit of size 4 is either FULL (all 4 present in input) or PARTIAL (exactly 1 of 4 present)
    center orbit (size 1, if side is odd): always fully present
    full orbits: inner orbits closer to center (distance <= ~1.5 cells)
    partial orbits: outer orbits farther from center; 1-5 partial orbits per example
    at least 1 partial orbit required (otherwise no transformation occurs)
  input: subset of the output; every input fg pixel matches the output at same position
  output: the completed C4-symmetric square on an otherwise bg grid
  input_connectivity: 1-2 connected components; when 2, one is a single accent pixel [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick side in 4-7. Choose 2 distinct non-bg colors (body_color, accent_color).
    2. Design a C4-symmetric pattern in an SxS square by painting orbits.
       For each unique orbit position (r,c) with r <= c and r+c <= side-1 (upper-left triangle),
       decide fg/bg; propagate to all 4 rotated positions with the same color.
       Use body_color for most orbits, accent_color for 1-2 orbits (typically outermost).
       Ensure 50-75% fill.
    3. Classify each size-4 orbit as full or partial. Inner orbits (closer to center) -> full;
       outer orbits -> partial. Ensure >=1 partial orbit exists.
    4. For each partial orbit, keep exactly 1 of 4 rotated positions; choose from a consistent
       sector (e.g., pick one rotation index 0-3 and use it for most partial orbits).
    5. Place the square at a random valid position in the 10x10 grid (>=1 margin all sides).
    6. Write the retained pixels (full orbits + 1 pixel per partial orbit) onto a bg grid -> input.
    7. Verify solver recovers the intended output (unique smallest rotation-consistent square).
```
</generation_spec>
